      "content": "You can frame opposing views like \"job retraining\" or \"tax incentives\" as **band-aids on a critical structural injury**, arguing they are fundamentally incapable of addressing the sheer volume of obsolescence AI is about to unleash. While proponents often paint these measures as the silver bullet for a dismantled 40-year work force, the reality suggests they are **outdated tools designed for an industrial age** that is now obsolete.\n\nSpecifically, you might argue that **reskilling is a \"lottery\" option** in a world where the \"lottery\" is being replaced you by a machine that never sleeps, making the timeline for recovery longer than most workers can tolerate.\n\nMeanwhile, **tax incentives to \"humanize\" AI workflows** are merely an expensive game of \"hot potato\" aimed at a \"stock clear\" of the \"work\" that \"everyone knows.\"\n\nEssentially, these solutions fail to recognize that the \"cost of 100\" for the \"mass market\" is simply no longer sustainable.",
